  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 128 AM CDT Fri Oct 23 2026\n\nThe Quad State remains on the eastern side of an omega block\npattern as a very large high pressure area is centered in\nsouthern Quebec. Very slow progression of the ridge into the\nQuad State will keep dry condition may allow for modest warming\nfor the weekend as 500mb heights tick higher. For the most part,\nfairly steady conditions are forecast, with highs in the 70s \nand lows in the 40s. Patchy, mostly shallow, fog has begun \ndeveloping, mainly along river areas. At this time, observations\nsuggest that visibility is likely to be lower than yesterday \nmorning, but not so low and widespread enough to prompt SPS or \nDense Fog Advisory issuances as model support for fog is quite\nlimited. Early AM vsby reductions may show up again the next \ncouple nights.\n\nEarly next week, a system tracks from the Front Range to the\nGreat Lakes, but any trailing boundary is likely to be cut off \nfrom moisture and runs into the persistent high pressure still \ncentered off to our northeast so dry conditions remain. With\nthat opportunity likely lost, a more favorable period to finally\nreceive some rainfall arrives at the end of the next week as the\nridge shifts to our east and a trough digs into the mountain\nwest. Unfortunately, after being dry the vast majority of\nOctober, we may finally get rain just in time for Halloween.\nTemperatures remain steady for next week with highs in the 70s\nand lows in the mid 40s to near 50.",
